from catalog import L, save
L(2,'01','Count to two hundred','Numbers 1–200',[13],
'Count, read, write and order numbers to 200.',
'Musa labels seed packets for a class project. What comes after ninety-nine?',
'After 99 comes 100. Ten tens make one hundred. Count 98, 99, 100, 101, 102. Count in tens from 100 to 200.',
'136 means 1 hundred, 3 tens and 6 ones. 200 means 2 hundreds and no tens or ones.',
'Make a track from 90 to 110 on scrap paper. A partner covers a number. Name it and explain. Then use place-value cards to show numbers to 200.',
'hundred; place value; order','Use ten ten-bundles for a hundred before formal hundreds notation.',
'Write one hundred and twenty-four in digits.=>124||Complete: 198, 199, __.=>200||Show 172 as hundreds, tens and ones.=>1 hundred, 7 tens, 2 ones||Order: 150, 105, 115.=>105, 115, 150||Count in tens from 160 to 200.=>160, 170, 180, 190, 200||Why does 108 have a zero?=>It has no tens between its hundred and eight ones.')
L(2,'02','Halves and quarters of sets','Fractions',[13],
'Find one half, one quarter and three quarters.',
'Amina shares tops equally among teams. What makes sharing fair?',
'To find half, share equally into 2 groups. To find a quarter, share equally into 4 groups. Three quarters means take 3 of those 4 equal groups.',
'12 tops make 4 equal groups of 3. One quarter of 12 is 3. Three quarters of 12 is 3 + 3 + 3 = 9.',
'Share 8 then 12 tops into two and four equal groups. Point to one half, one quarter and three quarters. Label fractions on folded paper too.',
'half; quarter; three quarters','The denominator counts equal groups, not objects in one group.',
'Half of 8 = __.=>4||One quarter of 8 = __.=>2||Three quarters of 8 = __.=>6||One quarter of 12 = __.=>3||Draw four groups of 2 dots. Ring three groups. How many ringed dots?=>6||Explain three quarters of 12.=>Share into four groups of 3 and take three groups, giving 9.','setquarters')
L(2,'03','Add without exchanging','Addition',[14],
'Add two- and three-digit numbers without regrouping.',
'Zainab counts books on two shelves. How can columns keep the totals clear?',
'Line up hundreds, tens and ones. Add each column. No exchange is needed when each column total is below ten. Estimate first by considering the tens.',
'123 + 45: ones 3 + 5 = 8; tens 2 + 4 = 6; one hundred stays. Total 168.',
'Use place-value mats to add two numbers without an exchange. Start with totals below 200. Then try 123 + 214 = 337 with three-digit addends.',
'column; hundreds; exchange','A shorter number needs an empty hundreds place, not a shift into another column.',
'34 + 25 = __.=>59||112 + 36 = __.=>148||123 + 214 = __.=>337||120 + 70 = __.=>190||43 books and 24 books make how many?=>67 books||Explain the ones in 123 + 45.=>3 ones plus 5 ones make 8; no exchange is needed.')
L(2,'04','Make a new ten','Addition',[14],
'Add two-digit numbers with regrouping.',
'Musa counts 8 loose strips and 7 more. What happens when the ones reach ten?',
'When there are 10 or more ones, exchange 10 ones for 1 ten. Write the remaining ones. Add the new ten to the tens column.',
'28 + 17: 8 + 7 = 15 ones = 1 ten and 5 ones. Tens: 2 + 1 + 1 = 4. Total 45. Check: 45 − 17 = 28.',
'Build 26 and 18 with paper bundles. Join, exchange ten loose strips for one bundle, and record 44.',
'regroup; exchange; total','Say what is exchanged: ten ones for one ten. Keep the new ten visible.',
'27 + 16 = __.=>43||38 + 24 = __.=>62||46 + 37 = __.=>83||59 + 25 = __.=>84||18 tops and 15 more make how many?=>33 tops||Why does 8 + 7 give an extra ten?=>15 ones contain one group of ten and five ones left.','regroup')
L(2,'05','Add three amounts','Addition',[14],
'Add three numbers in stages and check.',
'Daniel records three teams’ points. Which two numbers could he add first?',
'For three addends, add two first, then the third. Choose an easy pair. Keep every amount in the calculation exactly once.',
'18 + 12 + 7: first make 30, then 37. Check another order: 12 + 7 = 19; 19 + 18 = 37.',
'Draw three number cards. Build the total with place-value strips. Ask a partner to check in a different order.',
'addend; stages; total','Choosing pairs can ease mental work; never omit the third addend.',
'12 + 8 + 5 = __.=>25||23 + 17 + 9 = __.=>49||15 + 15 + 15 = __.=>45||42 + 18 + 20 = __.=>80||Teams score 16, 14 and 8. Total?=>38 points||Which pair helps in 27 + 3 + 6? Why?=>27 and 3 make 30, then add 6 to get 36.')
L(2,'06','Subtract tens and ones','Subtraction',[15],
'Subtract two-digit numbers without regrouping.',
'There are 58 paper strips. Amina uses 23. How can she separate tens and ones?',
'Align place-value columns. Subtract ones from ones and tens from tens. Check by adding the difference to the amount removed.',
'58 − 23: 8 − 3 = 5 ones; 5 − 2 = 3 tens. Answer 35. Check 35 + 23 = 58.',
'Make a stock of 47 strips. Remove 12 in bundles and loose ones. Show the remaining 35.',
'difference; subtract; check','Read the story before choosing an operation. Ask what remains and what was removed.',
'46 − 21 = __.=>25||78 − 34 = __.=>44||95 − 50 = __.=>45||67 − 6 = __.=>61||59 pages: read 24. How many left?=>35 pages||Check 86 − 42 = 44 using addition.=>44 + 42 = 86.')
L(2,'07','Exchange a ten to subtract','Subtraction',[15],
'Subtract two-digit numbers with regrouping.',
'Musa has 4 bundles and 2 loose strips. How can he remove 7 loose strips?',
'Exchange one ten for ten ones. The total value stays the same. Subtract the ones, then the remaining tens.',
'42 − 17: rewrite 42 as 3 tens and 12 ones. 12 − 7 = 5; 3 tens − 1 ten = 2 tens. Answer 25. Check 25 + 17 = 42.',
'Build 53. Exchange a bundle and remove 28. Record the stages. Check remaining 25 and removed 28 total 53.',
'exchange; difference; inverse','Do not subtract the smaller digit from the larger regardless of its position.',
'52 − 27 = __.=>25||71 − 36 = __.=>35||80 − 45 = __.=>35||63 − 18 = __.=>45||34 pencils: use 19. How many remain?=>15 pencils||Why can 52 be 4 tens and 12 ones?=>One of five tens becomes ten ones; total value stays 52.')
L(2,'08','Equal groups and multiplication','Multiplication',[16],
'Use repeated addition and multiplication notation.',
'Zainab puts equal numbers of tops on mats. How can she count equal groups?',
'Equal groups have the same number in each. In this book 3 × 4 means 3 groups of 4: 4 + 4 + 4. The sign × means multiplied by. An array has equal rows and columns.',
'3 groups of 4 tops = 4 + 4 + 4 = 12. So 3 × 4 = 12.',
'Make 2, 3, 4 or 5 equal groups. Write repeated addition and multiplication for each. Check by counting all objects.',
'groups; times; multiply; array','Keep group count and group size distinct in the story even when products match.',
'2 × 3 = __.=>6||4 + 4 + 4 = __.=>12||Write 5 + 5 as multiplication.=>2 × 5 = 10||4 groups of 2 tops contain how many?=>8||Draw 3 rows of 3 dots. How many?=>9||Why is 2 + 3 + 2 not three equal groups?=>One group has 3 and the others have 2.','array34')
L(2,'09','Missing-number puzzles','Open sentences',[17],
'Find unknowns and explain simple number relationships.',
'Daniel writes a sentence with a blank. How can his friends prove an answer?',
'Both sides of equals have the same value. Find a missing part with objects, counting or an inverse operation. Test the number in the original sentence.',
'__ + 15 = 42: use 42 − 15 = 27. Check 27 + 15 = 42. In 3 × __ = 12, the blank is 4.',
'Make a blank in a true sentence. Exchange puzzles with a partner. Describe the number relationship in words.',
'unknown; equal; inverse','Let pupils model a solution before explaining the symbols.',
'__ + 12 = 35.=>23||46 − __ = 21.=>25||__ − 18 = 24.=>42||3 × __ = 15.=>5||7 + 8 = __ + 5.=>10||How do you check __ − 9 = 16?=>Put 25 in the blank and verify 25 − 9 = 16.')
L(2,'10','Money and fair exchange','Money',[18],
'Name money uses and exchange equal values.',
'Amina makes a pretend shop. What could people use money for besides buying a toy?',
'Money can buy goods and services, be saved or be given. Naira is written ₦. Our pretend cards show ₦1, ₦2, ₦5, ₦10 and ₦20. They model values, not current notes or coins.',
'Two ₦5 cards equal ₦10. Four ₦5 cards equal ₦20.',
'Exchange paper value cards for equal totals. An adult may show genuine money and identify its value. Do not assume our value cards match issued denominations.',
'value; exchange; save','Historical scope uses amounts to ₦20. No present-day price claim is made.',
'How many ₦5 cards equal ₦10?=>2||₦10 + ₦5 + ₦2 = __.=>₦17||₦20 − ₦5 = __.=>₦15||Name one use of money.=>Buying goods or services, saving or giving.||Make ₦12 using ₦10 and ₦2 cards.=>One of each||Why is ₦10 worth more than three ₦2 cards?=>₦10 is greater than ₦6.')
L(2,'11','Our pretend shop','Money',[18],
'Buy and give change within ₦20.',
'Zainab labels pretend items for role-play. Who should check the change?',
'Add item values for the cost. Subtract cost from payment for change. Both buyer and seller check. All prices here are invented mathematical values.',
'Pretend costs: a card ₦6 and a paper shape ₦4. Total ₦10. Pay ₦20: change ₦10. Check ₦10 + ₦10 = ₦20.',
'Use paper objects and value cards. Take turns as buyer and seller. Keep cost and payment at or below ₦20.',
'cost; pay; change; receipt','Do not send pupils shopping for this task. Include oral or pointing roles.',
'Pretend costs ₦7 and ₦5. Total?=>₦12||Pay ₦20 for cost ₦12. Change?=>₦8||Pay ₦10 for cost ₦6. Change?=>₦4||Two pretend items cost ₦8 each. Total?=>₦16||Pay ₦20 for cost ₦16. Change?=>₦4||How can a buyer check the ₦8 change after ₦12 cost?=>Add ₦12 and ₦8 to get the ₦20 payment.')
L(2,'12','Why we need a metre','Length',[18],
'Compare body units and recognise metres and centimetres.',
'Musa and Amina get different span counts for the same table. Could both be careful?',
'Hand spans differ, so counts can differ. Standard units have agreed sizes. Metre is written m; centimetre is cm. 100 cm = 1 m. A ruler can measure short lengths; a metre strip measures longer ones.',
'A 30 cm ruler is useful for a book. A metre strip is useful for a table or classroom distance.',
'Two pupils measure a table with their own spans. Compare counts. Then use the same ruler or metre strip and explain the results.',
'metre; centimetre; standard','Never use a scaled printed ruler diagram as a physical ruler.',
'How many cm are in 1 m?=>100||Choose a convenient unit for a pencil: cm or m.=>cm||Choose a convenient unit for classroom length: cm or m.=>m||Write 1 m in cm.=>100 cm||Measure a book with a real ruler. Record cm.=>Practical reading with correct zero alignment and cm unit.||Why can hand-span counts differ?=>Different people’s spans can have different lengths.')
L(2,'13','Whole hours and half hours','Time',[19],
'Read whole-hour and half-hour times.',
'Daniel looks at a clock. Which hand gives the minutes?',
'The long hand shows minutes; the short hand hours. At a whole hour the minute hand is at 12. At half past it is at 6; the hour hand is halfway to the next hour.',
'At 3:00 the short hand points to 3 and the long hand to 12. At 3:30 the short hand is halfway between 3 and 4, with the long hand at 6.',
'Make a paper clock with movable hands. Show whole and half hours for a partner to name.',
'hour; minute; half past','Distinguish hand lengths. Move the hour hand between hour marks at half past.',
'Write half past four in digits.=>4:30||At 7:00 where is the minute hand?=>12||At 9:30 where is the minute hand?=>6||Write six o’clock in digits.=>6:00||Read clock A in the diagram.=>3:00||Where is the hour hand at 2:30? Explain.=>Halfway between 2 and 3, because half an hour has passed.','clocks')
L(2,'14','Seven days in a week','Time',[19],
'Name and order weekdays.',
'The friends plan classroom jobs. How can they show which day follows another?',
'The days in order here are Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, Friday, Saturday, Sunday. A week has 7 days. After Sunday comes Monday again.',
'The day after Tuesday is Wednesday. The day before Monday is Sunday.',
'Make seven day cards. Put them in a circle and name the next day. This is a practice sequence, not a claim about every school timetable.',
'week; day; yesterday; tomorrow','Connect names to a real calendar. Respect different family routines.',
'How many days make a week?=>7||What follows Friday?=>Saturday||What comes before Thursday?=>Wednesday||Complete: Sunday, Monday, __.=>Tuesday||Today is Wednesday. What is tomorrow?=>Thursday||Why does Monday follow Sunday?=>The seven-day sequence repeats.')
L(2,'15','Order by weight','Weight',[19],
'Arrange objects from lightest to heaviest.',
'Amina compares a leaf, an eraser and a small stone. What evidence is needed?',
'Compare pairs on a balance. Record results before ordering all objects. Size alone does not determine weight.',
'Practice results: eraser heavier than leaf; stone heavier than eraser. Order: leaf, eraser, stone. These are stated results, not a rule for all stones.',
'Use three safe small objects and an adult-supervised balance. Predict, test, then change your order if evidence disagrees.',
'lightest; heaviest; compare','Record the measured order. Objects with the same name need not have the same weight.',
'In the practice results, which is lightest?=>leaf||Which is heaviest?=>stone||Which is in the middle?=>eraser||A is heavier than B. Which pan is lower?=>A’s pan||Test and order three safe objects.=>Order supported by observed results.||Why test a weight prediction?=>Appearance can be misleading.')
L(2,'16','How much can it hold?','Capacity',[20],
'Identify containers and order capacities.',
'Musa chooses a container for plant water. Does a taller container always hold more?',
'Capacity is how much a container can hold. Measure with the same small cup each time. Use level cupfuls. A short wide container may hold more than a tall narrow one.',
'Jug A holds 4 equal cups; B holds 6. B has greater capacity by 2 cups.',
'With adult help, use water or dry sand. Predict and measure three containers with one cup. Order from least to greatest capacity.',
'capacity; container; cupful','Do not drink or taste activity contents. Prevent spills and use unbreakable containers.',
'Which practice jug holds more?=>B||How many more cupfuls does B hold?=>2||Order 5, 2, 4 cups from least.=>2, 4, 5||Name a suitable water container.=>Suitable watertight jug, bucket or bottle.||Compare two safe containers using one cup.=>Recorded counts using a common cup unit.||Why use the same cup?=>So each unit has equal capacity.')
L(2,'17','Cover a surface','Area',[20],
'Compare surface areas and recognise standard square units.',
'Zainab covers two cards with paper squares. What must she avoid between squares?',
'Area measures flat surface coverage. Use equal squares without gaps or overlaps. More of the same squares means more area. A square centimetre is 1 cm on each side; write cm². A square metre is 1 m on each side; write m².',
'Grid A has 3 rows of 4 squares: 12 square units. A card using 8 of the same squares has less area.',
'Cover two cards with equal paper squares. Record and compare. Use a ruler to make 1 cm squares when possible. Discuss which unit suits a book cover or floor.',
'area; surface; square unit','Area counts covering units, not distance around the edge. The diagram is not necessarily actual size.',
'How many squares are in grid A?=>12||Cards use 6 and 9 of the same squares. Which has more area?=>The 9-square card||What does cm² name?=>square centimetres||Draw 2 rows of 3 equal squares. Count them.=>6 squares||Cover a card and record square units.=>Count matches actual equal-square cover.||Why must comparison squares be equal?=>Unequal unit sizes make counts unfair to compare.','area34')
L(2,'18','Faces, edges and corners','Solids',[21],
'Count cube/cuboid features and recognise cylinder curvature.',
'Daniel turns a box. How can he avoid counting a corner twice?',
'A polyhedron face is flat. An edge is where two faces meet. A vertex is a corner. Cube and cuboid each have 6 faces, 12 edges and 8 vertices. A cylinder has a curved surface and two flat circular faces.',
'Mark box corners with tabs. Count 4 on one end and 4 on the other: 8 vertices.',
'Explore a cube and non-cube box. Tab faces, edges and corners as you count. Compare with a cylinder and sphere. Find examples at home or school.',
'face; edge; vertex; curved','Describe curved surfaces rather than extending polyhedron edge conventions to curved solids.',
'How many cube faces?=>6||How many cuboid vertices?=>8||How many cube edges?=>12||Which has curvature: cube or cylinder?=>cylinder||Name a cylinder model at home.=>Suitable closed straight-sided cylindrical container.||How can you avoid recounting box edges?=>Mark edges, or count four top, four bottom and four joining edges.','solids')
L(2,'19','Triangles and square corners','Flat shapes',[22],
'Name flat shapes and recognise right angles.',
'Amina folds a paper corner. Which corners in the room match it?',
'A triangle has 3 straight sides and 3 corners. A right angle is a square corner. Squares and rectangles each have 4 right angles. A triangle may have one right angle, but need not.',
'A rectangle stays a rectangle when turned. Fit a folded paper square corner against its corner to test the angle.',
'Trace circle, square, rectangle and triangle shapes. Sort by sides and right angles. Use a paper square corner to test.',
'triangle; right angle; straight','Not every triangle corner is a right angle. Use varied triangle shapes.',
'How many straight triangle sides?=>3||How many right angles in a rectangle?=>4||How many circle corners?=>0||Draw a triangle with one right angle.=>Three-sided shape with one square corner.||Name a shape with 4 equal sides and 4 right angles.=>square||How do triangle and rectangle differ?=>Triangle has 3 sides; rectangle has 4 and four right angles.','shapes')
L(2,'20','Collect, group and arrange','Data',[23],
'Collect information and organise groups and arrays.',
'Musa asks which of three games friends prefer. How can he record one choice per child?',
'Choose a question and categories. Record one answer per person. Group matching answers. Place one counter per response in an array of aligned rows.',
'Invented choices: ball, skipping, ball, clapping, ball, skipping. Counts: ball 3; skipping 2; clapping 1. Total 6.',
'Use the invented choices or a voluntary class question. Put a counter in its category for each answer. Align rows to compare counts.',
'category; response; array; data','Avoid sensitive questions. Total counters must equal total responses.',
'How many chose ball?=>3||How many chose skipping?=>2||How many responses altogether?=>6||Which game has fewest choices?=>clapping||Draw labelled rows of 3, 2 and 1 dots.=>Ball 3, skipping 2, clapping 1.||Why one choice per child for this question?=>Each participant is counted once, making counts comparable.')
